# Every warning a check of a binding rests on, raised again on the
# compiler's own lines wherever a header's pragma may have turned it off
# (design.md §4.19; panel 205's R1, ratified 2026-10-10; defect 570).
#
# **A pragma outranks the command line**, for the rest of the unit (GCC's and
# clang's documentation, panel 205's historian). So a group's header writing
# `#pragma clang diagnostic ignored "-Wsign-conversion"` with no push or pop
# turned the build's `-Werror=sign-conversion` off for every line the compiler
# wrote after it, and `abs(x: u32)` against C's `int abs(int)` built and
# printed `5` where the same binding through a plain header is refused. The
# sitting measured fourteen rows of that cause on this Mac and Linux arm64: a
# sign, a handle of another tag, an integer for a pointer (`read_p(p: i64)`
# against `int *`, aborting 134 at run time), a callback and an undeclared
# function, each under `ignored`, `GCC diagnostic ignored`, a downgrade to
# `warning`, `-Wconversion`, `-Weverything` and a push with no pop; and the
# same through a header macro the compiler's own line expands, `_Pragma` in a
# constant's value or in the bound name itself.
#
# **So the checks are raised again where a header's text can have run before
# a line that rests on them**: after the groups' includes
# (`emit/macro_guard.hero`), at the opening of the probe region
# (`emit/extern_probe.hero`), and after the accessor of every group constant,
# whose `return NAME;` expands the header's macro inside the definitions,
# before a call's `const` lend (`emit/body.hero`, `emit/record_constant.hero`).
# A name other than a constant's, which the compiler writes inside a probe or
# a body, is asked of clang instead and refused when its expansion holds a
# `_Pragma` (`cli/pragma_ask.hero`): a raise after it cannot reach the line it
# stands on.
#
# **The list is read, never copied**: each warning the build's flags make an
# error (`cli/flags.hero`'s one list, so a word added there is raised here
# too), and the three clang makes errors by default that a check rests on, an
# integer declared where the header takes a pointer, a function the header
# never declares, a callback of another type. Each is already an error on the
# command line or by default, so a raise changes nothing for a header that
# leaves the checks alone.

use ast
use source
use ir/containers
use emit/externs as emit_externs
use emit/writer
use cli/flags
use cli/strings

# The warnings clang makes errors by default, which a header can turn off
# like any other and which the build's flags do not name.
constant DEFAULT_ERRORS: [str]
    [
        "-Wint-conversion"
        "-Wimplicit-function-declaration"
        "-Wincompatible-function-pointer-types"
    ]

# Every warning a check rests on: each `-Werror=` word of the build's flags,
# in their order, then the default errors.
function checked() -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for word in flags.flags()
        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: "-Werror=")
            out @ out.push("-W" + word.slice(from: 8, to: word.len()))

    return strings.with_all(list: out, more: DEFAULT_ERRORS)

# One `error` pragma per warning a check rests on, as the unit writes them.
function lines() -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for word in checked()
        out @ out.push("#pragma clang diagnostic error \"" + word + "\"")

    return out

# After the accessor of a constant a group's header holds, the checks raised
# again: its `return NAME;` expanded the header's macro, and a `_Pragma` in it
# reaches every line after (the compiler's own headers' constants are the
# runtime's, and stay as they are).
function after_constant(@w: writer.Writer, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], f: containers.Function)
    header = emit_externs.extern_spans(decls, f).header

    if header.is_err() || emit_externs.is_own(emit_externs.unquoted(s, span: header.must()))
        return
    writer.at_generated(@w)

    for raised in lines()
        writer.line(@w, text: raised)

## Tests

# Every `-Werror=` word of the build's flags is raised, and the three default
# errors after them: a word the flags gain is raised with no edit here.
test "the checks raised are the build's -Werror words and clang's three default errors"
    got = checked()
    werror: [str] @ []

    for word in flags.flags()
        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: "-Werror=")
            werror @ werror.push(word)

    assert got.len() == werror.len() + 3
    assert got[0] == "-Wreturn-type"
    assert got[werror.len()] == "-Wint-conversion"
    assert got[got.len() - 1] == "-Wincompatible-function-pointer-types"

    for word in ["-Wsign-conversion", "-Wshorten-64-to-32", "-Wincompatible-pointer-types", "-Wincompatible-pointer-types-discards-qualifiers", "-Wimplicit-function-declaration"]
        found: bool @ false

        for one in got
            if one == word
                found @ true

        assert found

    assert lines()[0] == "#pragma clang diagnostic error \"-Wreturn-type\""
    assert lines().len() == got.len()
